  "details": "<p>This\u00a0<span class=\"highlightedSearchTerm\">archive</span>\u00a0is located in the\u00a0Northwest Palace.</p>\r\n<p>\u00a0<img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"328\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan66_general_plan_Kalhu1-17_p144.png\" width=\"354\" /></p>\r\n<p><img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"479\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan69_northwest_palace_Kalhu6-11_p149.png\" width=\"364\" /></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Most documents found in the Northwest Palace, built by the Assyrian king Ashurnarsipal II (883\u2013859 BC), predate the move of the new Assyrian capital to Dur-\u0160arrukin under Sargon II (721\u2013705 BC). Inside the well of Room AB, British excavators found the leaves of approximately thirty ivory and wooden writing boards, thus constituting the largest and best-preserved discovery of writing boards in Mesopotamia. According to the Akkadian text preserved on one of the objects, some of the writing boards contain copies of the astrological series En\u016bma Anu Enlil belonging to \u201cthe palace of Sargon (II).\u201d",
